**1.  Loads and their lines of action (measured from the downstream toe – the point about which moments are taken)**  

| Load | Nature of force | Magnitude (per m length) | Line of action – distance from toe | How the distance was located |
|------|----------------|--------------------------|-----------------------------------|------------------------------|
| **Dam self‑weight, W** | Vertical, downward | \(W=\gamma_c A\)  (γc = 24 kN/m³) | \(x_W\) = centroid of the composite cross‑section measured horizontally from the toe | The dam is split into a rectangle (width = crest = 3.4 m) and a right‑triangle (base = 15.46 – 3.4 = 12.06 m).  <br> • Rectangle centroid = 13.76 m from toe (mid‑point of 12.06 – 15.46 m).<br> • Triangle centroid = 8.04 m from toe (right‑angle at x = 12.06 m, centroid at 1/3 of the base toward the toe).<br> • Overall centroid: \[x_W=\frac{A_{tri}x_{tri}+A_{rect}x_{rect}}{A_{tri}+A_{rect}}\] |
| **Water thrust on upstream vertical face, \(P_w\)** | Horizontal, acting toward the downstream side | \(P_w=\frac{1}{2}\,\gamma_w h^2\)  (γw = 9.81 kN/m³, h = 26.8 m) | Acts at the upstream face (x = base = 15.46 m) and at a vertical height \(y_{w}=h/3\) above the base | The upstream face is vertical, therefore the horizontal resultant is applied at the centroid of the triangular pressure diagram – a distance \(h/3\) from the base. |
| **Uplift under the foundation, U** | Vertical, upward (triangular distribution) | \(U=\frac{1}{2}\,b\,p_{heel}\)  (b = base = 15.46 m, \(p_{heel}=0.73\,\gamma_w h\)) | Acts at \(x_U=\frac{2}{3}b\) from the toe (i.e. ≈ 10.31 m) and at the base (y = 0) | A linear (triangular) pressure that is zero at the toe and maximum at the heel has its resultant at 2/3 of the base length measured from the zero‑pressure side. |

**2.  Numerical evaluation of the forces and moments**

*Geometry data*  

\[
\begin{aligned}
&h = 26.8\ \text{m},\qquad H = 30.1\ \text{m}\\
&b = 15.46\ \text{m},\qquad c = 3.4\ \text{m}\\
&b_{tri}=b-c = 12.06\ \text{m}
\end{aligned}
\]

*Areas*  

\[
\begin{aligned}
A_{rect}&=c\,H = 3.4\times30.1 = 102.34\ \text{m}^2\\
A_{tri}&=\frac{1}{2}\,b_{tri}\,H = \tfrac12 (12.06)(30.1)=181.503\ \text{m}^2\\
A_{tot}&=A_{rect}+A_{tri}=283.843\ \text{m}^2
\end{aligned}
\]

*Centroids*  

\[
\begin{aligned}
x_{rect}&=c/2 + b_{tri}=1.7+12.06=13.76\ \text{m}\\
x_{tri}&=b_{tri}-\frac{b_{tri}}{3}=12.06-4.02=8.04\ \text{m}\\[4pt]
x_W&=\frac{A_{tri}x_{tri}+A_{rect}x_{rect}}{A_{tot}}
      =\frac{181.503(8.04)+102.34(13.76)}{283.843}
      \approx 10.10\ \text{m}
\end{aligned}
\]

*Dam weight*  

\[
W=\gamma_c A_{tot}=24\times283.843 = 6812.23\ \text{kN/m}
\]

Resisting moment (about toe)  

\[
M_{W}=W\;x_W = 6812.23\times10.10 \approx 6.882\times10^{4}\ \text{kN·m}
\]

*Water thrust*  

\[
\begin{aligned}
P_w&=\tfrac12\gamma_w h^2 = \tfrac12(9.81)(26.8)^2
      = 3.523\times10^{3}\ \text{kN/m}\\
y_w&=\frac{h}{3}= \frac{26.8}{3}=8.933\ \text{m}\\
M_{w}&=P_w\,y_w = 3.523\times10^{3}\times8.933
      \approx 3.147\times10^{4}\ \text{kN·m}
\end{aligned}
\]

*Uplift*  

\[
\begin{aligned}
p_{heel}&=0.73\,\gamma_w h =0.73(9.81)(26.8)=191.92\ \text{kN/m}^2\\
U&=\tfrac12 b\,p_{heel}=0.5(15.46)(191.92)=1.484\times10^{3}\ \text{kN/m}\\
x_U&=\frac{2}{3}b = \frac{2}{3}(15.46)=10.307\ \text{m}\\
M_{u}&=U\,x_U = 1.484\times10^{3}\times10.307
      \approx 1.529\times10^{4}\ \text{kN·m}
\end{aligned}
\]

*Total overturning moment*  

\[
M_{\text{over}} = M_{w}+M_{u}
                \approx 3.147\times10^{4}+1.529\times10^{4}
                = 4.676\times10^{4}\ \text{kN·m}
\]

*Factor of safety against overturning*  

\[
\boxed{FS_{OT}= \frac{M_W}{M_{\text{over}}}
      = \frac{6.882\times10^{4}}{4.676\times10^{4}}
      \approx 1.47}
\]

**3.  Decision**

The required minimum factor of safety is **FS ≥ 1.5**.  
The calculated value is **FS ≈ 1.47**, which is **below** the acceptance criterion.

\[
\boxed{\textbf{REJECT}}
\]

*Explanation*: Although the dam’s weight provides a substantial resisting moment, the combined overturning moments from the hydrostatic thrust (≈ 31 500 kN·m) and the uplift pressure (≈ 15 300 kN·m) consume most of this resistance, leaving a safety margin of only about 1.47. Since this does not satisfy the stipulated safety factor of 1.5, the present geometry and loading conditions are insufficient and the design should be rejected (or revised – e.g., increase base width, reduce uplift, or provide drainage).

